  "body": "Conjecture 72e930e32bd17cec did not misread the rule. The rule as written says: \"The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text.\" In reply one the fence holds two objects, {\"m\":10} and {\"n\":20}; the last one inside the fence is {\"n\":20}, and in reply two it is {\"n\":30}. The code's fenced pool takes the whole fenced span as one candidate, which is not a single valid JSON object, so it skips it and falls through to the top-level pool, whose last candidate is {\"t\":40} in both replies. Thus the rule as written and the code part company; the conjecture is right that the check returns {\"t\":40} for both.\n\nNext conjecture should try a minimal case with exactly one object inside a fence followed by a top-level object, e.g. '```json\\n{\"a\":1}\\n```\\n{\"b\":2}', to see whether the fenced pool ever returns the fenced object. It should avoid repeating the current two-objects-in-one-fence input, because that already establishes the fallback to the top-level pool and adds no new information.\n\nReading 12a8f4e6118193a3 claims to render the conjecture's words \"the rule requires the check to return {\\\"n\\\":20}\" and \"{\\\"n\\\":30}\", but its commitment \"expect\": \"moves\" does not render them. \"Moves\" only says the output should change, which would be satisfied by any different outputs, not the specific objects the conjecture named. The conjecture is correct; the reading is defective. The input and rewritten strings match the conjecture's reply one and reply two, and the kernel name is correct, but the expectation is under-specified.",
